{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Sqrt","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.period_eq_zero_of_all_residues_vanish","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.period_eq_zero_of_all_residues_vanish","kind":"theorem","line":225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L225-L232","doc":"**Hence no enclosed set `E` can produce a nonzero period.** A sum of zeros\nover any finite index set is zero, so the choice of `E` is immaterial: the\nquestion \"which branch points does the contour enclose\" has no content under\nthe classical-jet prolongation `λ_SW^(n) = X^(n) dz`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.chain_factor_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.chain_factor_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L216-L223","doc":"**The same fact as a chain product, for the general-`n` bookkeeping.**\n`n` applications of `(1/(2t)) d/dt` multiply the term `a_j t^j` by\n`∏_{i<n} (j − 2i)`; at the critical `j = 2n − 2` (writing `n = m+1`) the factor\nat `i = m` is zero. Kept because the probe reports this product across `n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.ddz_iterate_residue_slot_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.ddz_iterate_residue_slot_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L210-L214","doc":"Iterating changes nothing: the outermost application is already evaluated at\n`e = −2`, so the whole jet tower has vanishing residue. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.ddz_residue_slot_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.ddz_residue_slot_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L195-L207","doc":"**The residue slot is annihilated by a single application of `d/dz`.**\n\n`(ddz f)(−2) = ((−2) + 2) • f 0 = 0 • f 0 = 0`, for **every** `f`. The `t^{−2}`\ncoefficient of a `z`-derivative is always zero, because the only input that could\nreach it is the *constant* term `f 0`, and differentiating a constant kills it.\n\nSince `λ_SW^(n) = X^(n) dz = X^(n) · 2t dt`, the residue at a simple branch point\nis (up to the nonzero factor `2`) exactly this coefficient. So **every residue of\n`λ_SW^(n)` vanishes, at every branch point, for every `n ≥ 1`, whatever the\nPuiseux data** — the conclusion needs no global primitive and no compact surface,\nonly the multiplier `e + 2` at `e = −2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.is_root_of_w","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.is_root_of_w","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L157-L171","doc":"The root really is a root of `z² − wz + 1`, so the modulus computation above\nis about the branch points and not about an unrelated complex number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.normSq_root_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.normSq_root_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L144-L155","doc":"**A real `w` with `w² < 4` gives branch points of modulus exactly one.**\n\nThe roots of `z² − wz + 1` are `(w ± i√(4−w²))/2`, whose squared modulus is\n`(w² + (4−w²))/4 = 1`. So the bar-fixed branch points sit *on* the unit\ncircle — and the unit circle is therefore **not** an admissible contour. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.w_real_root_mem_Ioo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.w_real_root_mem_Ioo","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L135-L142","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L133-L133","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic_neg_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic_neg_two","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L127-L131","doc":"**The real root lies in `(−2, −1)`** — hence in `(−2, 2)`, which is what puts\nits two branch points exactly on the unit circle. Proved from two integer\nevaluations, `wCubic (−2) = −4 < 0` and `wCubic (−1) = 23 > 0`, by the\nintermediate value theorem. No numerics. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.wCubic","kind":"def","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L124-L125","doc":"The reduced cubic, over `ℝ`, whose roots are the `w`-values of the branch points. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.w_cubic_of_palindromic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.w_cubic_of_palindromic","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L115-L122","doc":"The `w = z + 1/z` reduction of the palindromic sextic: `Δ(z)/z³` equals\n`16w³ + 24w² − 13w + 2` at `w = z + z⁻¹`. Verified as an identity in `z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.branchLocus_inv_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.branchLocus_inv_mem","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L104-L113","doc":"**The branch locus is closed under the bar involution `z ↦ 1/z`.**\n\nThis is the Hecke bar-involution `q ↦ q⁻¹` acting on the pole locus of\n`λ_SW^(n)`. It constrains any admissible enclosed set `E`: bar carries poles\ninside the unit circle to poles outside it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.disc_inv_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.disc_inv_mul","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L97-L102","doc":"**Self-reciprocity.** `z⁶ · Δ(1/z) = Δ(z)` for `z ≠ 0` — the algebraic form\nof \"the coefficient list reads the same backwards\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.disc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicBranchLocus.disc","kind":"def","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicBranchLocus.lean#L91-L95","doc":"The colour cubic's discriminant, up to the overall sign that does not affect\nits zero set: `Δ₆(z) = 16z⁶ + 24z⁵ + 35z⁴ + 50z³ + 35z² + 24z + 16`. The\ncoefficient list is palindromic, which is the whole content of this section. ","signatureChunk":0}]}